@meta
id: 06-05
title: Drill — both ends of the line
chapter: 06
type: drill
skills: line-start, line-end
par: 68
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: Home, type, End, type, Down — four times, eight times over
open: seed.ts

@brief
Four seed calls. Each one needs `await ` at the front of the code and a `;`
at the end. The dojo will hand you the file eight times.

Nothing to work out. The rep is `^` to the front, insert, `$` to the back,
insert, `j` to the next line. Front, back, down.

@keys
| `^` | first non-blank |
| `$` | last character |
| `i` `a` | insert before, insert after |
| `j` | down a line |

@hints
`^` `i` `await ` `Esc` does the front. `$` `a` `;` `Esc` does the back.
--
Do not look at the screen between the two halves. The point of the drill is
that both anchors become one press each rather than a decision.

@solution
    j then, four times: ^ i await  <Esc> $ a ; <Esc> j

Both ends of a line cost exactly one key each, and after eight passes they
should cost no thought either. That matters more than it looks: once `^` and
`$` are free you stop scrolling your eyes along a line to work out where you
are, and start treating every line as something with a front and a back that
you can be at instantly.

`I` and `A` fuse each pair into a single key and you should use them when
you are only inserting. Keep the separate motions in your fingers anyway —
from chapter 8 they take operators, and `d$` is a different animal to `A`.

@start seed.ts
export async function seed() {
  seedOrder('ord_1042', 'cus_88', 4999)
  seedOrder('ord_1043', 'cus_91', 1250)
  seedOrder('ord_1044', 'cus_88', 899)
  seedOrder('ord_1045', 'cus_92', 15750)
}

@expected seed.ts
export async function seed() {
  await seedOrder('ord_1042', 'cus_88', 4999);
  await seedOrder('ord_1043', 'cus_91', 1250);
  await seedOrder('ord_1044', 'cus_88', 899);
  await seedOrder('ord_1045', 'cus_92', 15750);
}
